title: Change Healthcare / ALPHV
page: breaches/change-healthcare-alphv.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: ALPHV logged in to a Change Healthcare Citrix portal that had no MFA, moved laterally for nine days, then deployed ransomware; UnitedHealth's final count is about 192.7 million people affected.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 192.7 | million affected | about, UnitedHealth's final count; the largest US healthcare breach
stat: 1 | missing control: MFA | on an internet-facing remote access portal, confirmed to Congress
stat: 9 | days from initial access to ransomware | February 12 to February 21, 2024
stat: $22M | ransom paid, UnitedHealth confirmed | to the Senate Finance Committee
Change Healthcare processes around 15 billion healthcare transactions a year and touches roughly one in three US patient records.
notes:
Start with the four headline facts the page leads with.

On February 12, 2024, the ALPHV/BlackCat ransomware group used compromised credentials to log in to a Change Healthcare Citrix remote access portal. The portal did not have multi-factor authentication, MFA, enabled. UnitedHealth's chief executive confirmed to Congress that the portal lacked MFA. The page puts it as one account, on one internet-facing portal, missing one control.

The actors moved laterally and exfiltrated data for nine days, then deployed ransomware on February 21.

Change Healthcare processes around 15 billion healthcare transactions a year and touches roughly one in three US patient records, so the outage did not stay technical. Parts of the US healthcare payment system stopped working for weeks.

UnitedHealth paid a $22 million ransom, which it confirmed to the Senate Finance Committee, and the payment was followed by a second extortion attempt on the same data. In 2025 UnitedHealth put the final count at about 192.7 million individuals affected, the largest healthcare data breach in US history.

Keep the attributions when you say these numbers aloud. UnitedHealth itself confirmed the payment, and the final count is UnitedHealth's: say about 192.7 million.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: February 12, 2024 | Stolen credentials open a Citrix remote access portal with no MFA
event: February 12 to 21 | Lateral movement and data exfiltration, for nine days
event: February 21 | Ransomware deployed
event: For weeks after | Parts of US healthcare payments stop; some services degraded for months
event: Date not given | UnitedHealth pays a $22 million ransom; a second demand follows
event: 2025 | UnitedHealth puts the final count at about 192.7 million affected
The page names the ALPHV/BlackCat ransomware group as the attacker. It gives no date for the payment or the second demand; those markers follow the page's order, not a known sequence. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page dates February 12 and February 21, 2024, and gives a year, 2025, for the final count. The timeline shows only those dates, with the durations and outcomes the page gives without dates.

On February 12, 2024, the actors used stolen credentials to access a Citrix remote desktop portal that had no MFA. For nine days they moved laterally and exfiltrated data. On February 21 they deployed ransomware.

Then came the impact. Pharmacies could not verify coverage, providers could not submit claims or get paid, and parts of the US healthcare payment system stopped working for weeks. The page says some services were degraded for months.

UnitedHealth paid a $22 million ransom, which it confirmed to the Senate Finance Committee. The payment did not end it: the data resurfaced under a second extortion demand. In 2025 UnitedHealth put the final count at about 192.7 million individuals affected.

The page gives no date for the payment or the second demand, so do not supply one. Those two markers follow the page's order, not a known sequence. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

== Who said what, and how firm it is
layout: table
row: Claim | Source on the page | How firm
row: No MFA on the portal | UnitedHealth's chief executive | Confirmed to Congress
row: $22 million ransom paid | UnitedHealth | Confirmed to the Senate Finance Committee
row: About 192.7 million affected | UnitedHealth | Its final count, put in 2025
row: Exit scam within ALPHV | Public reporting; the affiliate's claim | Apparent
Whether the credentials came from an infostealer log, a prior breach or a purchase was not conclusively established publicly.
notes:
Slow down on this slide. The page is careful about who stands behind each claim and how firm it is, and the deck keeps those differences.

Two claims rest on UnitedHealth's own confirmation. Its chief executive confirmed to Congress that the portal lacked MFA, and UnitedHealth confirmed the $22 million ransom payment to the Senate Finance Committee.

The count is UnitedHealth's too: in 2025 it put the final count at about 192.7 million individuals affected.

The exit scam rests on public reporting. The affiliate who conducted the intrusion claimed the core group took the ransom and disappeared. The page calls it an apparent exit scam, and draws its lesson for payment decisions whatever the internal truth.

Where the credentials came from is open. The page says whether they came from an infostealer log, a prior breach or a purchase was not conclusively established publicly. An infostealer is malware that scrapes saved credentials and session cookies from an infected machine.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | Stolen credentials, no MFA | T1133
step: 02 | Lateral Movement | Nine days, then ransomware | T1021
step: 03 | Impact | Change Healthcare offline | T1486
step: 04 | Impact | Paid, then a second demand | T1657
Arrows follow the page's stage order. Stages 03 and 04 both sit under Impact. UnitedHealth confirmed the payment; the page gives it no date.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's four stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, Initial Access, is the login with stolen credentials to a Citrix portal that had no MFA. The page maps it to T1133, External Remote Services, and also to T1078, Valid Accounts.

Stage 02, Lateral Movement, is the nine days of movement and exfiltration before the ransomware.

Stages 03 and 04 both sit under the page's Impact phase. Stage 03 is Change Healthcare offline, and the healthcare payments that stopped with it. Stage 04 is the $22 million ransom UnitedHealth paid, which it confirmed to the Senate Finance Committee, and the second demand that followed it.

The page gives no date for the payment, so read the last arrow as the page's order rather than as a time. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Stolen credentials opened a Citrix portal that had no MFA
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1133 | External Remote Services
technique: T1078 | Valid Accounts
fact: Date | February 12, 2024
fact: Surface | a Citrix remote desktop portal, internet-facing
fact: Missing control | MFA, confirmed in congressional testimony
fact: Credential source | not conclusively established publicly
There was no exploit in this step: a valid username and password was sufficient.
notes:
Stage 01 is the way in. The page maps it to T1133, External Remote Services, and T1078, Valid Accounts.

On February 12, 2024, the actors used stolen credentials to access a Citrix remote desktop portal that had no multi-factor authentication enabled. UnitedHealth's chief executive confirmed to Congress that the portal lacked MFA.

The page stresses that there is no exploit in this step and no sophistication. A valid username and password, typed into a remote access gateway exposed to the internet, was sufficient.

It also says why such portals draw attackers. The page calls remote access portals the most consistently targeted surface in ransomware operations, precisely because they are designed for access from anywhere, so an attacker holding credentials is indistinguishable from an employee working from home.

Whether the credentials came from an infostealer log, a prior breach or a purchase was not conclusively established publicly. What was established, the page says, is that a second factor would have made the answer irrelevant.

The next slide explains External Remote Services and MFA in general terms.

== What External Remote Services covers, and what MFA adds
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1133 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133/
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary, MFA (csoh.org)
ATT&CK's T1133 covers adversaries using external-facing remote services, such as VPNs and Citrix, to gain initial access to a network. Using the service often requires a valid account. MFA pairs something you know with something you have, or are.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK and the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes External Remote Services, T1133, as adversaries leveraging external-facing remote services to initially access, or persist within, a network. Remote services such as VPNs, Citrix and other access mechanisms let users connect to internal enterprise network resources from external locations. ATT&CK adds that there are often remote service gateways that manage connections and credential authentication for these services.

It also notes that access to valid accounts is often a requirement for using the service.

A VPN is a virtual private network.

The csoh.org glossary defines MFA, multi-factor authentication, as something you know plus something you have, or something you are.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-mfa

== Nine days of lateral movement and exfiltration, then ransomware
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1021 | Remote Services
technique: T1041 | Exfiltration Over C2 Channel
fact: Dwell time | February 12 to February 21: nine days, then ransomware
fact: In that window | lateral movement, privilege escalation, bulk data exfiltration
fact: Double extortion | the stolen copy is leverage a backup cannot defeat
The page calls these nine days the part of this chain that offers defenders the most.
notes:
Stage 02 is the nine days between getting in and deploying ransomware. The page maps it to T1021, Remote Services, and T1041, Exfiltration Over C2 Channel. C2 is command and control, the channel an attacker uses to control what it has planted.

From February 12 to February 21, the actors moved laterally through the environment and exfiltrated data. On February 21 they deployed ransomware.

The page explains why the data came first. Modern ransomware operations are double extortion: the data has to be stolen before anything is encrypted, because the stolen copy is the leverage that survives a good backup.

Nine days is a long time, and the page calls it the part of this chain that offers defenders the most. The next slide shows why.

== Detect in the nine-day window, or only at encryption
layout: compare
col: In the window | Credential harvesting, network enumeration | Privilege escalation | Sustained outbound transfer of large volumes of data | Every one of those is detectable
col: Only at encryption | Encryption is the end of the attack, not the beginning | By then the data has already been stolen | The entire opportunity to intervene is skipped
Modern ransomware operations are double extortion: the data is stolen before encryption.
notes:
This slide draws the choice the page puts to defenders.

The page describes the window before encryption as quiet but active: credential harvesting, network enumeration, privilege escalation, and sustained outbound transfer of large volumes of data. Every one of those is detectable. For this intrusion, the page records lateral movement, privilege escalation and bulk data exfiltration in the window, and calls it nine days of anomalous internal and outbound activity.

The right-hand column is what detecting only at encryption means. Encryption is the end of the attack, not the beginning, and the page says an organization that only detects at the encryption stage has skipped past its entire opportunity to intervene. Because modern ransomware operations are double extortion, the data has already been stolen by then.

A defense later in the deck turns this into hunting. The next slide explains lateral movement in general terms.

== Lateral movement and Remote Services
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1021 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary, Lateral Movement (csoh.org)
Lateral movement is pivoting from one foothold to other systems. ATT&CK's T1021 covers adversaries using valid accounts to log in to services that accept remote connections, then acting as the logged-on user.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary and MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

The glossary describes lateral movement as pivoting from one foothold to other systems.

ATT&CK's Remote Services technique, T1021, describes adversaries using valid accounts to log in to a service that accepts remote connections, such as telnet, SSH and VNC, and then performing actions as the logged-on user. SSH is secure shell, and VNC, Virtual Network Computing, shares a remote screen.

ATT&CK adds that in an enterprise environment, servers and workstations can be organized into domains, which provide centralized identity management. An adversary who obtains a set of valid domain credentials could log in to many different machines using remote access protocols such as SSH or the remote desktop protocol, RDP.

These are general descriptions. The page does not say which services, protocols or accounts the actors used to move.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-lateral-movement

== Coverage checks, claims and provider payments stopped with Change Healthcare offline
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Impact
technique: T1486 | Data Encrypted for Impact
fact: Volume | ~15 billion healthcare transactions per year
fact: Reach | roughly one in three US patient records
fact: Duration | weeks of disruption, some services degraded for months
fact: Concentration | no alternative, and no visibility into the dependency
This chain's impact is measured in whether a patient could collect a prescription.
notes:
Stage 03 is the impact. The page maps it to T1486, Data Encrypted for Impact.

Change Healthcare processes around 15 billion healthcare transactions a year and touches roughly one in three US patient records. With it offline, pharmacies could not verify coverage, and providers could not submit claims or receive payment. The page puts the duration at weeks of disruption, with some services degraded for months.

The page says every other chain on the site measures impact in records, dollars or systems, and this one is measured in whether a patient could collect a prescription.

Change Healthcare sits in the middle of US healthcare payments as a clearing house, so taking it offline did not degrade one company but interrupted a function that thousands of independent providers and pharmacies depend on and have no alternative for. A healthcare clearing house is an intermediary that processes claims and related transactions between providers and health plans, that is, insurers.

The next slide shows what that meant for pharmacies and providers.

== What the outage meant for pharmacies and providers
layout: compare
col: Pharmacies | Could not verify coverage | Fell back to manual processes | Or turned patients away
col: Providers | Could not submit claims or receive payment | Small practices went weeks without revenue | Some borrowed to make payroll
Thousands of independent providers and pharmacies depend on the function Change Healthcare performs, and have no alternative for it.
notes:
This slide puts the impact in the terms the page uses.

Pharmacies could not verify coverage. They fell back to manual processes or turned patients away.

Providers could not submit claims or receive payment. Small practices went weeks without revenue, some borrowing to make payroll.

The page calls this concentration risk in its most literal form. Efficiency had routed a third of a country's patient transactions through a single processor, and no participant in that system had chosen or could see that dependency.

The page gives no count beyond thousands, so do not put a more exact number on them.

The next slide explains blast radius, the term in the page's phase label, and the ATT&CK technique behind this stage, in general terms.

== What blast radius means, and what T1486 covers
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, Blast Radius | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-blast-radius
credit: Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1486 (attack.mitre.org)
Blast radius is the scope of damage if a credential, identity or system is compromised; minimizing it is a design principle. ATT&CK's T1486 covers encrypting data to interrupt availability, as in ransomware, which seeks payment for decryption.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary and MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

The glossary defines blast radius as the scope of damage if a credential, identity or system is compromised, and calls minimizing it a design principle.

ATT&CK describes Data Encrypted for Impact, T1486, as adversaries encrypting data on target systems, or on large numbers of systems in a network, to interrupt availability to system and network resources. They can render stored data inaccessible by encrypting files or data on local and remote drives and withholding access to a decryption key.

ATT&CK says this may be done to extract monetary compensation from a victim in exchange for decryption or a decryption key, which is ransomware, or to render data permanently inaccessible where the key is not saved or transmitted.

Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1486: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486/

== UnitedHealth paid a $22 million ransom; a second demand followed
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Impact
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Payment | $22 million, confirmed by UnitedHealth to the Senate Finance Committee
fact: Within ALPHV | an apparent exit scam; the affiliate said it was not paid
fact: Then | the data resurfaced under a second extortion demand
Whatever the internal truth, paying buys a promise from a criminal organization that may not control its own affiliates, and data can be monetized more than once.
notes:
Stage 04 is the money. The page maps it to T1657, Financial Theft.

UnitedHealth paid a $22 million ransom, which it confirmed to the Senate Finance Committee. The payment did not end it. Public reporting described an exit scam within the ALPHV operation: the affiliate who conducted the intrusion claimed the core group took the ransom and disappeared. An exit scam is when the people running an operation vanish with money owed to others.

After that, the stolen data appeared again in the hands of another extortion group, demanding payment a second time.

Keep the page's caveat, whatever the internal truth. The lesson it draws for anyone weighing a payment decision is concrete: paying buys a promise from a criminal organization that may not control its own affiliates, and the data can be monetized more than once.

UnitedHealth put the final count at about 192.7 million affected individuals, which the page calls the largest healthcare data breach in US history.

The next slide explains T1657 in general terms.

== What T1657, Financial Theft, covers
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1657 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1657/
ATT&CK's T1657 covers adversaries stealing money through extortion, social engineering, technical theft or other methods. In ransomware extortion, an adversary may demand payment after encrypting and exfiltrating data, threatening to leak it unless paid.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Financial Theft as adversaries stealing monetary resources from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft or other methods aimed at their own financial gain, at the expense of the availability of those resources for victims.

It names extortion by ransomware among several campaign types whose ultimate objective is financial theft, alongside business email compromise and fraud.

Its example of ransomware extortion is an adversary demanding payment from a victim after Data Encrypted for Impact and exfiltration of data, followed by threatening to leak sensitive data to the public unless payment is made.

These are general descriptions of the technique, not an account of this incident.

== One account, one portal, one missing control
layout: compare
col: The way in | One account | One internet-facing portal | One missing control: MFA
col: The reach | ~15 billion healthcare transactions a year | Roughly one in three US patient records | Parts of the US healthcare payment system stopped for weeks
The page says efficiency had routed a third of a country's patient transactions through a single processor, and calls that concentration risk.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws in its own summary.

On one side, the way in. The page says the initial access was one account, on one internet-facing portal, missing one control. It says there was no exploit in that step and no sophistication.

On the other side, the reach. Change Healthcare processes around 15 billion healthcare transactions a year and touches roughly one in three US patient records, so the outage did not stay technical, and parts of the US healthcare payment system stopped working for weeks.

The page calls the second side concentration risk in its most literal form. Efficiency had routed a third of a country's patient transactions through a single processor, and no participant in that system had chosen or could see that dependency.

Next, the page's defenses, set against the stages.

== Six defenses against four stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04
row: MFA on every internet-facing access path | 01
row: Hunt in the pre-encryption window | 02
row: Segment so one foothold cannot reach everything | 02
row: Know your concentration risk | 03
row: Plan for the continuity of those who depend on you | 03
row: Decide your ransom position before you need it | 04
A mark means the defense's own words name that stage. The page calls the MFA defense the entire chain in one sentence; its instructions name stage 01's surface.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its four stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something from that stage.

Enforcing MFA names stage 01's surface: remote access surfaces, Citrix among them, and the old systems nobody owns, which the page says is exactly what was exploited here. The page calls this defense the entire chain in one sentence. The matrix marks it at stage 01, where its instructions land.

Hunting in the pre-encryption window names stage 02's nine days. Segmentation names stage 02 too: nine days of lateral movement, the page says, implies a flat enough environment to permit it.

Knowing your concentration risk names stage 03: thousands of providers discovered their revenue depended on a company they had no relationship with. Planning for the continuity of those who depend on you names the same harm, which fell mostly on organizations with no ability to influence Change Healthcare's security.

Deciding your ransom position names stage 04: the payment here was followed by a second extortion attempt on the same data.

Every column has a mark. That reflects what the defenses name, not a judgment of how well each would have worked.

== Enforce MFA on every internet-facing access path
layout: text
- List every remote access surface: VPN, Citrix, RDP gateways, VDI, legacy portals.
- Verify MFA on each, rather than assuming it because policy says so.
- Exceptions are usually old systems nobody owns; the page says that is what was exploited here.
notes:
This defense answers stage 01, and the page calls it the entire chain in one sentence.

Enumerate every remote access surface you expose: VPN, Citrix, RDP gateways, VDI and legacy portals. Then verify that MFA is enforced on each one, rather than assuming it because policy says so.

The page says the exceptions are usually old systems nobody owns, which is exactly what was exploited here.

A VPN is a virtual private network. RDP is the remote desktop protocol, and VDI is virtual desktop infrastructure.

== Hunt before encryption, and segment so one foothold cannot reach everything
layout: compare
col: Hunt the window (stage 02) | Detection tuned only to ransomware execution arrives at the end | Alert on credential dumping, unusual internal reconnaissance, new admin accounts | And on mass file access and sustained outbound transfer volumes
col: Segment (stage 02) | Nine days of lateral movement implies a flat enough environment | Separate clinical, administrative and payment systems; re-authenticate across them | Restrict which accounts reach systems that would halt operations
The page says segmentation converts a total outage into a partial one.
notes:
Two defenses answer stage 02.

First, hunt in the pre-encryption window, because it is nine days wide. Detection tuned only to ransomware execution arrives at the end of the story. Alert on the behaviours that precede it: credential dumping, unusual internal reconnaissance, new admin accounts, mass file access, and sustained outbound transfer volumes. The page points to its detection engineering topic for this.

Second, segment so that one remote access foothold cannot reach everything. Nine days of lateral movement implies a flat enough environment to permit it. Separate clinical, administrative and payment systems, require re-authentication crossing those boundaries, and restrict which accounts can reach the systems that would halt operations. The page says segmentation converts a total outage into a partial one.

The page does not say which systems the actors reached, so present the flat environment as the page does: as what nine days of lateral movement implies.

== Your dependencies, and who depends on you
layout: compare
col: Your dependencies (stage 03) | Map the third parties that would stop your operations if they stopped | Find which have no substitute | Build a manual or alternate process for the critical ones
col: Those who depend on you (stage 03) | The harm fell mostly on organizations with no ability to influence Change Healthcare's security | Plan for their continuity: a duty to your customers' customers | Publish enough during an incident for them to make decisions
Thousands of providers discovered their revenue depended on a company they had no relationship with.
notes:
Two defenses answer stage 03.

First, know your own concentration risk, including the vendors your vendors depend on. Thousands of providers discovered their revenue depended on a company they had no relationship with. Map the third parties that would stop your operations if they stopped, identify which have no substitute, and build a manual or alternate process for the critical ones. The page says that mapping is the work, and the alternate process is the outcome.

Second, if you operate infrastructure others depend on, plan for their continuity too. The harm here fell mostly on organizations with no ability to influence Change Healthcare's security. If you are that kind of provider, the page says, your resilience planning is a duty to your customers' customers, and publishing enough during an incident for them to make decisions is part of it.

== Decide your ransom position, and assume payment may not resolve anything
layout: text
- Here, a second extortion attempt on the same data followed the payment.
- Settle in advance who decides and what legal and regulatory constraints apply.
- Know what recovery without paying looks like: tested, immutable, offline backups.
notes:
This defense answers stage 04.

Decide your ransom position before you need it, and assume payment may not resolve anything. The payment here was followed by a second extortion attempt on the same data.

Establish in advance who decides, what legal and regulatory constraints apply, and what your recovery looks like without paying. The page says that means tested, immutable, offline backups, and points to its backup and DR topic.

DR is disaster recovery. An immutable backup is one that cannot be modified or deleted before its retention window expires.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can you list every internet-facing access path and show MFA enforced on each?
check: Would you alert on credential dumping, new admin accounts or mass file access?
check: Would sustained outbound transfer volumes be noticed before encryption?
check: Could one remote access foothold reach the systems that would halt your operations?
check: Which third parties have no substitute, and is there a manual process for each?
check: Is it settled who decides on a ransom, and is recovery without paying tested?
check: If others depend on you, what would you publish for them during an incident?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether you could list every internet-facing access path (VPN, Citrix, RDP gateways, VDI, legacy portals) and show MFA enforced on each, rather than assumed from policy. Pay attention to old systems nobody owns.

Ask whether anything would alert on credential dumping, unusual internal reconnaissance, new admin accounts, mass file access or sustained outbound transfer volumes, before encryption. Ask whether one remote access foothold could reach the systems that would halt your operations.

Ask which third parties would stop your operations if they stopped, which have no substitute, and whether a manual or alternate process exists for the critical ones.

Ask whether it is settled who decides on a ransom, what legal and regulatory constraints apply, and whether recovery without paying has been tested.

Finally, if others depend on you, ask what you would publish during an incident so they can make decisions.

== Which of your remote access paths would accept a password alone?
layout: question
A valid username and password, typed into an internet-facing Citrix portal with no MFA, was sufficient.

Name one remote access path in your environment, and say how you know it enforces MFA.
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page stresses that stage 01 held no exploit and no sophistication. A valid username and password, typed into a remote access gateway exposed to the internet, was sufficient, and UnitedHealth's chief executive confirmed to Congress that the portal lacked MFA.

Ask each person to name one specific path, and to say how they know MFA is enforced there: because they checked, or because policy says so. The answer slide gives what the page says about finding the paths you have forgotten.

== What the page says about forgotten access paths
layout: answer
The page calls this defense the entire chain in one sentence.
- Enumerate VPN, Citrix, RDP gateways, VDI and legacy portals.
- Verify MFA on each, rather than assuming it because policy says so.
- Exceptions are usually old systems nobody owns.
notes:
The page's answer is to enforce MFA on every internet-facing access path, and to audit for the ones you have forgotten. It calls this the entire chain in one sentence.

Enumerate every remote access surface you expose (VPN, Citrix, RDP gateways, VDI, legacy portals) and verify MFA is enforced on each, rather than assuming it because policy says so.

The page says the exceptions are usually old systems nobody owns, which is exactly what was exploited here.

Bring the group's examples back to this. If someone knows a path enforces MFA only because policy says so, the page's point applies to it.

== Which third party would stop your operations if it stopped?
layout: question
Thousands of providers discovered their revenue depended on a company they had no relationship with.

Name one dependency like that in your operations, including a vendor your vendors depend on.
notes:
This question comes from stage 03 and the concentration risk defense.

Change Healthcare sits in the middle of US healthcare payments as a clearing house. Thousands of independent providers and pharmacies depend on the function it performs and have no alternative for it. The page says no participant in that system had chosen or could see that dependency.

Ask each person to name one dependency in their own operations, and to say whether it has a substitute. Let the group include the vendors their vendors depend on before you show the answer slide.

== What the page says about concentration risk
layout: answer
That mapping is the work; the alternate process is the outcome.
- Map the third parties that would stop your operations if they stopped.
- Identify which have no substitute.
- Build a manual or alternate process for the critical ones.
notes:
The page's answer is to know your own concentration risk, including the vendors your vendors depend on.

Map the third parties that would stop your operations if they stopped. Identify which have no substitute, and build a manual or alternate process for the critical ones. The page says that mapping is the work, and the alternate process is the outcome.

If anyone in the room operates infrastructure others depend on, the page adds a second duty: plan for their continuity too, and publish enough during an incident for them to make decisions.

Bring the group's examples back to this. A dependency with no substitute and no manual process is the case the page describes.

== Sources
layout: links
link: Cybersecurity Dive, no MFA on the Citrix portal | https://www.cybersecuritydive.com/news/change-healthcare-compromised-credentials-no-mfa/714792/
link: BleepingComputer, the stolen Citrix account | https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/change-healthcare-hacked-using-stolen-citrix-account-with-no-mfa/
link: TechTarget, breached via Citrix portal | https://www.techtarget.com/searchsecurity/news/366582824/Change-Healthcare-breached-via-Citrix-portal-with-no-MFA
link: MITRE ATT&CK T1133 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133/
notes:
These are the primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

Cybersecurity Dive and BleepingComputer are the two sources linked in the page's header: Cybersecurity Dive on the compromised credentials and the missing MFA, and BleepingComputer on the stolen Citrix account. TechTarget's report is on the breach via the Citrix portal. The MITRE page defines T1133, External Remote Services, behind stage 01.

The background slides name their own sources: MITRE ATT&CK and the csoh.org glossary.

== Related defense topics
layout: links
link: Backup, DR and ransomware | backup-dr.html
link: IAM and cloud identity | iam.html
link: Detection engineering | detection-engineering.html
link: Incident response | incident-response.html
The page names no related kill chain. These are its related defense topics on csoh.org.
notes:
The page links no related kill chain, so this slide carries its four related defense topics on csoh.org.

Backup, DR and ransomware is the topic the ransom defense points to for tested, immutable, offline backups. Detection engineering is the topic the hunting defense points to. IAM and cloud identity, and incident response, are the other two.

DR is disaster recovery, and IAM is identity and access management.
